in this episode of cloud performance  analysts we take a look at a service  that powers billion user systems  BigTable will we set enough places for  performance stay tuned to find out when  it comes to performance at scale there's  very few products that truly represent  that term the way that cloud BigTable  does to date BigTable is a technology  behind the majority of Google products  including Gmail maps and YouTube each of  which serves multi billion active users  and since BigTable is part of Google  cloud platform it's available to use in  your applications as well but before you  run off into the weeds switching over  your database to use it we need to take  a look at how things work and show how  it should and shouldn't be used so let's  take a look under the hood as a massive  generalization cloud BigTable can be  described as three systems working  together first you have a front-end  server pool second you have a set of  compute nodes that handle connections a  KA the BigTable cluster and thirdly you  have a scalable backend of storage now  see a client request goes through a  front-end server pool before they are  sent to the cloud BigTable node  therefore each node in the cluster  handles a subset of requests for the  entire system and the front ends will  handle balancing these connections  depending on the type of action that the  connection is interested in and what  table it's wanting to work on to store  the underlying data in each of your  tables cloud BigTable shards the data  into multiple tablets where each tablet  contains a contiguous range of rows  within the table I know a lot of  vernacular and here's the important  thing when it comes to tablets they can  be reassigned to different nodes in your  cluster on-demand allowing BigTable to  scale and rebalance seamlessly as your  usage patterns change for example as  reads and writes to a specific tablet  increase cloud BigTable might split it  in two in order to isolate hot rows and  improve performance from here you can  clearly see that BigTable does not act  like other relational databases and more  to the point while it scales amazingly  well and isolates hotspots gracefully  that doesn't make it a perfect use case  for every scenario see cloud BigTable  excels at handling very large amounts of  data like terabytes over relatively long  periods of  timelike days this is because big table  needs time to learn your access patterns  and the data needs time to be large  enough to warrant usage across all of  the nodes in your cluster this makes it  a perfect fit for things like time  series data marketing data low latency  serving financial data IOT data and  graph data basically systems with lots  of data and contiguous usage now if your  data needs are small like less than 300  gigs or if your interactions last for  short periods of time then check out the  article linked in the description below  where we list some other technologies  that might be a better fit for your  application likewise don't forget to  subscribe to our YouTube channel for  more great cloud performance Atlas  content because when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

